<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pushyabhuti dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 647 CE (approx.) · capital Kannauj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dynasty of Harsha, who ruled much of northern India from Kannauj in the 7th century and was described by the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Pushyabhutis ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thanesar-state">Thanesar</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, from around 500. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabhakaravardhana">Prabhakaravardhana</a> made them a regional power in the late 6th century. After his death his elder son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajyavardhana">Rajyavardhana</a> was murdered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashanka">Shashanka</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gauda-kingdom">Gauda Kingdom</a>, and in 606 his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> took the throne, also inheriting the kingdom of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maukharis">Maukharis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> campaigned across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a> stopped him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>. He governed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kings, toured his realm and held great religious assemblies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayag</a>. A patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> and Sun cults, he wrote three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> plays, and his court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabhatta">Banabhatta</a> composed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harshacharita">Harshacharita</a>. Harsha exchanged embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> died in 647 without an heir, and his empire collapsed at once. A minister seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> broke into rival states until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara</a> rose in the 8th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pushyabhuti-dynasty.jpg" alt="Pushyabhuti dynasty" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harshavardhana_Circa_AD_606-647.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 647 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kannauj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Prabhakaravardhana, Rajyavardhana, Harsha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Harsha's empire, Vardhana dynasty, Empire of Harsha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=574&amp;focus=pushyabhuti-dynasty" aria-label="Pushyabhuti dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6783 1186 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pushyabhuti dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7103 1442L7253 1492M7103 1442L7103 1442M7103 1442L7362 1550M7103 1442L6930 1626"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7253 1492h0M7103 1442h0M7362 1550h0M6930 1626h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7103 1442h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7103 1442h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kannauj. <a href="/atlas?year=574&amp;focus=pushyabhuti-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
